---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import CompareTable from "../../components/CompareTable.astro";
import CtaBand from "../../components/CtaBand.astro";

const rows = [
  { label: "Works on product images", them: true, us: false },
  { label: "Works on product descriptions, articles and pages", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Invisible to customers", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Mark travels with a copy of what it protects", them: "For images", us: true },
  { label: "Survives cropping or removal tools", them: false, us: "Survives editing and trimming" },
  { label: "Identifies a copy found on another site", them: false, us: true },
  { label: "Produces an ownership document for a takedown", them: false, us: true },
];
---

<Base
  title="Kirpik vs visible watermarks"
  description="Visible image watermarks solve an image problem, at a visible cost. There is no visible equivalent for text, and a copyright footer is not protection. What invisible text watermarking does instead."
>
  <PageHero
    title="Visible watermarks vs invisible text protection"
    lead="A logo stamped across a product photo travels with every copy of that photo, and that is genuinely useful. But it answers an image question. Your descriptions, articles and pages need a different answer, because no visible mark can ride along inside a sentence."
  />

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow prose">
      <h2>What visible watermarking gets right</h2>
      <p>
        Ten apps in Shopify's anti-theft category stamp a visible logo, text or badge onto product
        images, several of them in bulk with automatic application and restorable originals. Against the
        blockers that dominate the category, this approach has one honest advantage: the mark is part of
        the file. Save the image, screenshot it, re-upload it, and the mark is still there. The
        protection travels, which is exactly what a storefront script cannot offer.
      </p>

      <h2>What it costs, and where it ends</h2>
      <p>
        The price is paid in the open. Every customer sees the mark on every photo, which is why these
        apps compete on making marks subtle, and why store owners agonise over opacity sliders. And the
        mark can be taken off: a corner logo crops out, and inpainting tools reconstruct the pixels
        underneath a mark laid across the whole frame. A visible mark deters and brands; it does not
        bind.
      </p>
      <p>
        More fundamental for this site's subject: it is an image technique. The description next to the
        photo, the buying guide, the ingredient story that took a week to write, none of that is touched.
        In the whole ninety-app category we surveyed in June and July 2026, not one app applies any kind
        of watermark to text.
      </p>

      <h2>Why there is no visible watermark for words</h2>
      <p>
        You could try. A copyright line under every description? The thief's paste selection simply stops
        one line short, and a footer proves nothing anyway, since anyone can type one. Your store name
        woven into every paragraph? Find and replace removes it faster than you added it. Wording so
        distinctive it acts as a signature? That is just writing the thief will lightly edit. Anything a
        reader can see in text, an editor can delete, and text is edited far more casually than images
        are. A visible mark on words is either destroyed in the first thirty seconds or it was never
        really a mark.
      </p>

      <h2>The text-side equivalent is invisible</h2>
      <p>
        Kirpik gives text the property that makes image watermarks worth having, without the visible
        cost. It embeds an invisible algorithmic watermark, derived from your original text, into your
        product descriptions, blog articles and pages. Your words do not change, readers and search
        engines see nothing, and the signature repeats through the content with redundancy to spare. Copy
        it, trim it, reword a few lines, paste it into another theme: detection still reads your store's
        watermark code out of the copy, and a Proof of Ownership Certificate turns that into a dated,
        verifiable ownership document for a DMCA takedown.
      </p>
      <p>
        If your photos are your crown jewels too, a visible mark or an invisible image watermark is a
        sensible companion. The point is that words were the unprotected half, and visibility was never
        an option for them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top:0">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <CompareTable themLabel="Visible watermark" rows={rows} />
      <p class="tnote">
        The visible-watermark column reflects the typical image-watermark app in the Shopify anti-theft
        category, per each app's own public listing, June and July 2026. Removal notes describe what
        cropping and image-editing tools do in general. App names and trademarks belong to their owners.
        Not legal advice. Full survey at <a href="/compare/">the comparison hub</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <CtaBand
    heading="Your photos can wear a badge. Your words need a signature."
    sub="Invisible, algorithmic, derived from your original text, and there when you need to prove ownership. Free to start."
  />
</Base>

<style>
  .tnote { margin-top: 1rem; font-size: 0.84rem; color: var(--ink-faint); line-height: 1.65; }
  .tnote a { color: var(--orange-deep); font-weight: 600; }
</style>
